1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,720
How do you calculate
the current through a wire?

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:04,345
The first answer that comes to mind:

3
00:00:04,425 --> 00:00:07,020
divide the charge that passed
by the time it took.

4
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:10,535
But even though current flows
through the wire nonstop,

5
00:00:10,615 --> 00:00:12,790
can this division give you zero?

6
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:15,180
And without a single calculation error?

7
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,670
Yes, it can.

8
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:18,670
We'll see how in a moment.

9
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,110
By the end of this part, we'll have built
the real definition of current.

10
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:24,007
And with three examples,
we'll learn to tell

11
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:26,300
average current
from instantaneous current.

12
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:28,735
In the last one,
we'll see something surprising:

13
00:00:28,815 --> 00:00:30,971
the current can be zero
just when the charge

14
00:00:31,051 --> 00:00:32,950
through the wire is at its maximum.

15
00:00:42,658 --> 00:00:46,808
Hello everyone, welcome
to the Circuit Theory I series.

16
00:00:46,888 --> 00:00:50,318
Today, in the first part of Lecture 1,
we'll talk about current.

17
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:53,086
Whatever you design in circuit theory,

18
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:56,220
you start by defining
the quantities correctly.

19
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,390
That's exactly where this series begins.

20
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:00,530
Today's topic is charge and current.

21
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:03,630
We'll cover voltage in the third
part of this lecture.

22
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:05,130
First, charge.

23
00:01:05,210 --> 00:01:08,470
We denote charge by the letter q;
its unit is the coulomb.

24
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:09,772
In circuit theory,

25
00:01:09,852 --> 00:01:14,310
we treat charge as a quantity
we can count, add and subtract.

26
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:18,060
The electron's charge is negative,
the proton's is positive.

27
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:21,830
The deeper nature of charge
is a physics question, not our job.

28
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:26,390
Our question is different:
where does this quantity go, and how fast?

29
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:29,710
The moment charge moves,
current comes into play.

30
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:31,620
So how do we measure current?

31
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,380
Picture an imaginary
cross-section through the wire.

32
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:36,020
Let's put a counter on this cross-section.

33
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,859
The counter counts charge
passing to the right

34
00:01:37,939 --> 00:01:40,330
as positive and charge passing
to the left as negative.

35
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:41,530
Let's wait for a while.

36
00:01:41,610 --> 00:01:46,220
Let's call this time Δt, and the net
charge shown on the counter Δq.

37
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:50,570
The first definition that comes
to mind: current = Δq / Δt.

38
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:52,020
Seems reasonable, right?

39
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:53,210
But there's a problem.

40
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:55,450
How do we choose Δt?

41
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:56,910
Consider a wire like this.

42
00:01:56,990 --> 00:01:58,333
In the first half second,

43
00:01:58,413 --> 00:02:02,030
5 mC passes the cross-section
to the right, at a steady rate.

44
00:02:02,110 --> 00:02:05,587
In the next half second,
5 mC passes to the left this time,

45
00:02:05,667 --> 00:02:07,270
again at a steady rate.

46
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:12,920
If we choose a 1-second window,
the counter adds +5 and −5 and shows zero.

47
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,590
Δq / Δt comes out zero.

48
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:17,580
So is there no current in the wire at all?

49
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:19,530
There is, and it never stops.

50
00:02:19,610 --> 00:02:23,869
In the first half second, charge moves
right at a rate of 10 mC per second,

51
00:02:23,949 --> 00:02:25,720
then left at the same rate.

52
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:30,080
So the current is first
+10 mA, then −10 mA.

53
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,620
It's never zero at any instant.

54
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,980
We made no calculation error;
we just chose a bad window.

55
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:36,801
Charge kept passing
through the cross-section

56
00:02:36,881 --> 00:02:38,020
throughout that one second.

57
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,770
But because the net charge transferred
is zero, the average came out zero.

58
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:43,971
The wider the window,

59
00:02:44,051 --> 00:02:47,960
the more of what happens inside
gets squeezed into a single number.

60
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:49,850
What if we narrow the window?

61
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:53,710
If we place it inside the first
half second, we get 10 mA.

62
00:02:53,790 --> 00:02:57,194
But if we slide a window of the same
width right to the middle,

63
00:02:57,274 --> 00:03:00,910
onto the moment the charge reverses,
the result is zero again.

64
00:03:00,990 --> 00:03:03,738
Here we assume the reversal
is instantaneous;

65
00:03:03,818 --> 00:03:06,310
let's set that exact instant aside.

66
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:08,644
The problem is this:
a wide window mixes two

67
00:03:08,724 --> 00:03:11,050
different behaviors into a single number.

68
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:13,790
But a good definition should
describe a single instant.

69
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:16,320
The solution is to shrink
the window without limit.

70
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,290
dt is a tiny time
interval approaching zero.

71
00:03:19,370 --> 00:03:23,590
And dq is the tiny charge passing
the cross-section in that tiny time.

72
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:25,278
Here's the real definition of current:

73
00:03:25,358 --> 00:03:28,270
current is the rate at which charge
passes through a cross-section.

74
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:30,820
So i = dq / dt.

75
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:32,020
Its unit is the ampere.

76
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,610
If one coulomb of charge passes
per second, the current is one ampere.

77
00:03:35,690 --> 00:03:40,570
For example, if 2 µC passes every
millisecond, the current is 2 mA.

78
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:42,940
That means 2 mC per second.

79
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:47,050
With this definition, current is no longer
a single number but a function of time.

80
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:50,180
We can plot it and take its derivative.

81
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,740
Subscribe to the channel
to follow the rest of this series.

82
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:56,880
Did you notice? Current
is no longer an average.

83
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:00,670
It's a time-dependent quantity
with its own value at every instant.

84
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:03,540
Let's also take a quick look
at where this series is heading.

85
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,210
After charge and current,
we'll get to know voltage.

86
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:10,280
Then we'll study sources,
resistors and capacitors.

87
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:12,425
Next, we'll cover Kirchhoff's laws,

88
00:04:12,505 --> 00:04:16,490
the analysis methods built on them,
and the circuit theorems.

89
00:04:16,570 --> 00:04:21,643
After that, we'll move to circuits
made of a resistor and a capacitor,

90
00:04:21,723 --> 00:04:26,550
then a resistor and an inductor,
and then all three together.

91
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:29,530
Our last stop is operational amplifiers.

92
00:04:29,610 --> 00:04:33,110
We cover each lecture in three
parts of about 15 minutes each.

93
00:04:33,190 --> 00:04:36,410
All along the way, we'll use
current as we defined it today.

94
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:38,990
Now let's reinforce this
with three examples.

95
00:04:39,410 --> 00:04:40,850
Let's move on to our first example.

96
00:04:40,930 --> 00:04:44,060
Charge flows through a wire for 4 ms.

97
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:47,120
In the first 1 ms, 5 µC passes.

98
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:50,730
In the next 3 ms, only 3 µC.

99
00:04:50,810 --> 00:04:53,760
Within each interval,
the charge flows at a steady rate.

100
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:56,300
The question:
what is the current in this wire?

101
00:04:56,390 --> 00:04:59,580
The first instinct is to divide
the total charge by the total time.

102
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:04,250
8 µC / 4 ms = 2 mA.
The calculation is correct.

103
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:09,660
But was the current in the wire
actually 2 mA at any instant? Let's see.

104
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:14,960
In the first interval,
5 µC passed in 1 ms, which makes 5 mA.

105
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:16,633
There's a handy shortcut here:

106
00:05:16,713 --> 00:05:20,730
dividing microcoulombs by milliseconds
gives milliamperes directly.

107
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:25,920
In the second interval,
3 µC passed in 3 ms, which is 1 mA.

108
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:27,290
Now let's draw the graph.

109
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:30,110
The horizontal axis is time,
in milliseconds.

110
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:32,900
The vertical axis is current,
in milliamperes.

111
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:36,490
Between 0 and 1 ms, the current is 5 mA.

112
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:39,760
Between 1 and 4 ms, it's 1 mA.

113
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:42,150
From this, we can answer
two questions right away.

114
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:44,530
What is the current at 0.5 ms?

115
00:05:44,610 --> 00:05:45,620
5 mA.

116
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:47,830
What is the current at 3 ms?

117
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:50,910
1 mA. There's a single
answer for every instant.

118
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:54,470
Now let's pick a window
between 0 and 0.5 ms.

119
00:05:54,550 --> 00:06:00,000
In this time, 2.5 µC passes;
dividing by 0.5 ms gives 5 mA again.

120
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:03,744
As long as the window stays within
an interval where the current is constant,

121
00:06:03,824 --> 00:06:06,140
we get the same answer whatever its width.

122
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:09,880
Now let's choose the window
between 0 and 2 ms.

123
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:15,960
5 µC passes in the first millisecond
and 1 µC in the next, 6 µC in total.

124
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:18,760
Dividing by 2 ms gives 3 mA.

125
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,680
That isn't the value
at any instant either.

126
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:23,570
As we change the window,
the average changes too.

127
00:06:23,650 --> 00:06:25,500
So where is 2 mA?

128
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:29,060
Let's draw the 2 mA level
on the graph as a dashed line.

129
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:32,100
As you can see, the current never
takes this value at any instant.

130
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:35,610
2 mA is the average
current over these 4 ms.

131
00:06:35,690 --> 00:06:39,190
The average isn't wrong;
it just answers a different question.

132
00:06:39,270 --> 00:06:44,200
If you're asking how much total charge
passed in 4 ms, the average does the job.

133
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:47,672
But if you're asking how much current
is flowing through the wire right now,

134
00:06:47,752 --> 00:06:49,360
the average won't answer you.

135
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,530
The trap here is writing Δq / Δt without
checking what the question asks for.

136
00:06:54,610 --> 00:06:58,140
Is the question asking for the average,
or for the current at a specific instant?

137
00:06:58,220 --> 00:07:02,066
Also notice this: since the charge
flows steadily within each interval,

138
00:07:02,146 --> 00:07:05,446
if you choose the window inside
an interval where the current

139
00:07:05,526 --> 00:07:09,470
stays constant, Δq / Δt already
equals the instantaneous current.

140
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:12,150
Now a slightly harder example.

141
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,490
Here the current changes at every instant.

142
00:07:14,570 --> 00:07:18,083
The total charge passing
a wire's cross-section,

143
00:07:18,163 --> 00:07:22,270
starting from time zero,
grows like this: q = 2t² mC.

144
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:24,480
Here t is in seconds.

145
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:28,810
The question:
what is the current at t = 3 s?

146
00:07:28,890 --> 00:07:30,680
Let's try the average approach first.

147
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:33,530
Let's find how much charge
has passed in 3 seconds.

148
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:35,860
We substitute 3 for t in the formula.

149
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:39,240
3² = 9, and twice 9 is 18.

150
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:42,480
So 18 mC has passed in 3 seconds.

151
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:45,640
Average current is the charge
that passed divided by the time it took.

152
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:49,560
Dividing 18 mC by 3 seconds gives 6 mA.

153
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,223
Pause for a moment and guess:

154
00:07:51,303 --> 00:07:55,140
is the current at 3 seconds
greater or less than this average?

155
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,000
Now let's use the definition.

156
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:00,410
Current is the derivative
of charge with respect to time.

157
00:08:00,490 --> 00:08:02,160
The derivative of t² is 2t.

158
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,980
Multiplying that by 2, we get 4t.

159
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:07,360
So the current is 4t mA.

160
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:10,100
At 3 seconds, let's substitute 3 for t.

161
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:12,580
4 × 3 = 12 mA.

162
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:17,510
That's exactly twice the average,
because 12 divided by 6 is 2.

163
00:08:17,590 --> 00:08:21,250
Let's check the unit too: mC / s gives mA.

164
00:08:21,330 --> 00:08:23,270
This factor of two is no coincidence.

165
00:08:23,350 --> 00:08:26,536
If charge starts at zero
and grows with the square of time,

166
00:08:26,616 --> 00:08:29,095
the average current
from zero up to an instant

167
00:08:29,175 --> 00:08:31,820
is exactly half the current
at that instant.

168
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:36,040
Because the current starts at zero
and rises linearly with time.

169
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:39,860
So the average sits exactly halfway
between the initial and final values.

170
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:42,500
And halfway between 0 and 12 is 6.

171
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:44,640
We can see this without
taking a derivative, too.

172
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:49,390
Let's open a window starting
at 3 seconds and narrow it step by step.

173
00:08:49,470 --> 00:08:52,040
In each window,
we'll follow the same three steps.

174
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:55,480
First, we'll find the charge at the end
of the window from the formula.

175
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:59,310
Then we'll subtract the 18 mC
at 3 seconds from it.

176
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:01,430
This difference is the charge
that passed in the window.

177
00:09:01,510 --> 00:09:04,670
Finally, we'll divide this
difference by the window width.

178
00:09:04,750 --> 00:09:08,650
Let the window be 1 second,
from 3 to 4 seconds.

179
00:09:08,730 --> 00:09:12,180
4² = 16, twice that is 32 mC.

180
00:09:12,260 --> 00:09:15,770
Subtracting 18 from 32 leaves 14 mC.

181
00:09:15,850 --> 00:09:18,950
Dividing by 1 second gives 14 mA.

182
00:09:19,030 --> 00:09:22,050
Now let's shrink the window
to a tenth of a second.

183
00:09:22,130 --> 00:09:24,700
The window ends at 3.1 seconds.

184
00:09:24,780 --> 00:09:27,790
3.1² = 9.61.

185
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:31,020
Twice that is 19.22 mC.

186
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:34,600
Subtracting 18 leaves 1.22 mC.

187
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:39,010
Dividing by 0.1 seconds gives 12.2 mA.

188
00:09:39,090 --> 00:09:41,170
Now let's shrink the window
to a hundredth of a second.

189
00:09:41,250 --> 00:09:44,140
The window ends at 3.01 seconds.

190
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:48,240
3.01² = 9.0601.

191
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:52,210
Twice that is 18.1202 mC.

192
00:09:52,290 --> 00:09:56,650
Subtracting 18 leaves 0.1202 mC.

193
00:09:56,730 --> 00:10:02,010
Dividing by 0.01 seconds gives 12.02 mA.

194
00:10:02,090 --> 00:10:07,880
See? First 14, then 12.2, then 12.02.

195
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:11,840
As the window shrinks,
the result approaches 12 mA.

196
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:14,720
dq / dt is exactly this limiting value.

197
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:16,140
Let's see this on the graph too.

198
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:18,205
When we plot charge against time,

199
00:10:18,285 --> 00:10:23,210
the average current between two points
is the slope of the line joining them.

200
00:10:23,290 --> 00:10:24,940
We call this line a secant.

201
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:26,280
As the window narrows,

202
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:30,480
the secant approaches the tangent
to the curve at the 3-second point.

203
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:32,830
The slope of this tangent is 12 mA.

204
00:10:32,910 --> 00:10:36,550
So the instantaneous current is the slope
of the charge graph at that instant.

205
00:10:36,670 --> 00:10:37,830
Let's ask one more question.

206
00:10:37,910 --> 00:10:42,160
At what instant does the current
equal the average value, 6 mA?

207
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:46,210
Since the current is 4t, we write 4t = 6.

208
00:10:46,290 --> 00:10:49,520
Dividing 6 by 4 gives t = 1.5 seconds.

209
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:53,650
So the average value is actually a value
the current takes at some instant.

210
00:10:53,730 --> 00:10:57,870
But that instant isn't the 3 seconds
we asked about; it's 1.5 seconds.

211
00:10:57,950 --> 00:10:59,656
You can see it on the graph too:

212
00:10:59,736 --> 00:11:02,201
the tangent parallel
to the secant from 0 to 3

213
00:11:02,281 --> 00:11:04,800
touches the curve exactly at 1.5 seconds.

214
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:08,300
What if this current flowed
through a 2 kΩ resistor?

215
00:11:08,380 --> 00:11:10,669
By Ohm's law,
the voltage across the resistor

216
00:11:10,749 --> 00:11:13,120
is the product of resistance and current.

217
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:17,110
At 3 seconds: 2 kΩ × 12 mA.

218
00:11:17,190 --> 00:11:19,390
2 × 12 = 24.

219
00:11:19,470 --> 00:11:22,000
And kilohms times
milliamperes gives volts.

220
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:23,590
So 24 V.

221
00:11:23,670 --> 00:11:25,874
Had we used the average current,

222
00:11:25,954 --> 00:11:29,310
we'd have gotten 2 kΩ × 6 mA,
that is, 12 V.

223
00:11:29,390 --> 00:11:31,470
That's half the actual
voltage at that instant.

224
00:11:31,550 --> 00:11:33,780
So in this example,
the error in the current

225
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:36,160
carries straight over to the voltage.

226
00:11:36,650 --> 00:11:38,670
Our last example is a bit
thought-provoking.

227
00:11:38,750 --> 00:11:43,858
This time, the net charge through
the cross-section changes like this:

228
00:11:43,938 --> 00:11:45,490
q = (6t − t²) mC.

229
00:11:45,570 --> 00:11:49,820
t is again in seconds, and we look
at the interval from 0 to 4 seconds.

230
00:11:49,970 --> 00:11:51,170
First, a question for you.

231
00:11:51,250 --> 00:11:55,380
At 3 seconds, what is the charge
in mC, and the current in mA?

232
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:57,340
Pause and try to find it yourself.

233
00:11:57,420 --> 00:11:58,600
Let's start with the charge.

234
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:01,580
At 3 seconds, 6t is 18.

235
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:03,020
And t² is 9.

236
00:12:03,130 --> 00:12:06,420
Subtracting 9 from 18, the charge is 9 mC.

237
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:08,590
That's the maximum value in this interval.

238
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:10,430
Let's check this with numbers too.

239
00:12:10,510 --> 00:12:15,710
At both 2.5 seconds and 3.5 seconds,
the charge is 8.75 mC.

240
00:12:15,790 --> 00:12:21,230
On both sides of 3 seconds the charge is
smaller, so 3 seconds really is the peak.

241
00:12:21,310 --> 00:12:23,120
Now the current.
Let's take the derivative.

242
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,160
The derivative of the 6t term is 6,
and the derivative of t² is 2t.

243
00:12:27,240 --> 00:12:32,980
So the current is found
by subtracting 2t from 6: (6 − 2t) mA.

244
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:38,740
At 3 seconds, 2t is 6;
subtracting 6 from 6 gives 0 mA.

245
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:42,510
Interesting, isn't it? When the charge
is at its maximum, the current is zero.

246
00:12:42,590 --> 00:12:44,529
In fact, the slope
interpretation from the previous

247
00:12:44,609 --> 00:12:46,080
example tells us this right away.

248
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:48,850
The charge graph peaks at 3 seconds.

249
00:12:48,930 --> 00:12:51,710
At the peak, the tangent is horizontal,
so the slope is zero.

250
00:12:51,790 --> 00:12:55,860
Current depends not on how much charge
there is, but on how fast it changes.

251
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:57,310
Let's look at other instants too.

252
00:12:57,390 --> 00:13:02,690
At 1 second, 2t is 2; subtracting
2 from 6, the current is 4 mA.

253
00:13:02,770 --> 00:13:08,720
At 4 seconds, 2t is 8; subtracting
8 from 6, the current is −2 mA.

254
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:10,220
What does the minus sign mean?

255
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:12,930
After 3 seconds,
the charge starts to decrease.

256
00:13:13,010 --> 00:13:14,174
That means charge is now passing through

257
00:13:14,254 --> 00:13:15,800
the cross-section
in the opposite direction.

258
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:17,585
We'll see the direction of current,

259
00:13:17,665 --> 00:13:21,090
and how the sign encodes that direction,
in detail in Part 2.

260
00:13:21,170 --> 00:13:23,060
So when is the current largest?

261
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:26,510
The expression 6 − 2t
gets smaller as t grows.

262
00:13:26,590 --> 00:13:31,030
So the maximum is at the start,
when t is zero: 6 mA.

263
00:13:31,110 --> 00:13:32,790
And at that instant, the charge is zero.

264
00:13:32,870 --> 00:13:34,250
The picture has completely flipped.

265
00:13:34,330 --> 00:13:36,137
When the charge is zero,
the current is at its maximum;

266
00:13:36,217 --> 00:13:38,410
when the charge is at its maximum,
the current is zero.

267
00:13:38,490 --> 00:13:41,540
Now let's look at the average
between 0 and 4 seconds.

268
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:45,890
At 4 seconds, the charge
is 24 minus 16, which is 8 mC.

269
00:13:45,970 --> 00:13:48,350
Dividing 8 by 4 gives 2 mA.

270
00:13:48,430 --> 00:13:49,702
Yet in this interval,

271
00:13:49,782 --> 00:13:53,660
the current took very different
values, such as 4, 0 and −2.

272
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:57,420
The average squeezes this whole
story into a single number.

273
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:59,730
The trap here is assuming
that if the charge is large,

274
00:13:59,810 --> 00:14:01,380
the current must be large too.

275
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:04,940
We just saw it: when the charge was
at its maximum, the current was zero.

276
00:14:05,380 --> 00:14:06,980
Let's sum up.

277
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:11,760
Current is the rate at which charge passes
through a cross-section: i = dq / dt.

278
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:15,799
Δq / Δt, on the other hand, only gives
the average over the window you choose,

279
00:14:15,879 --> 00:14:19,430
and approaches the instantaneous
current as the window shrinks.

280
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:21,201
What determines current is not the amount

281
00:14:21,281 --> 00:14:23,620
of charge that passes,
but its rate of change.

282
00:14:23,810 --> 00:14:25,680
We saw the same thing
in all three examples.

283
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:29,200
With the step current, the average
wasn't the value at any instant.

284
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:30,645
With the steadily rising current,

285
00:14:30,725 --> 00:14:33,030
the instantaneous value
was twice the average.

286
00:14:33,110 --> 00:14:36,310
And in the last example, the current was
zero when the charge was at its maximum.

287
00:14:36,390 --> 00:14:39,850
When finding the voltage with Ohm's law,
we also used the instantaneous current.

288
00:14:39,970 --> 00:14:41,780
Let me leave you with a question.

289
00:14:41,860 --> 00:14:46,550
If the charge were (4t − t²) mC,
at what instant would the current be zero?

290
00:14:46,630 --> 00:14:48,640
Hint: think about the peak.

291
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:50,230
Write your answer in the comments.

292
00:14:50,380 --> 00:14:51,620
If this lecture helped you,

293
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:54,550
please like the video
and share it with your classmates.

294
00:14:54,630 --> 00:14:57,320
Subscribe to the channel
so you don't miss Part 2.

295
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:59,928
In Part 2, we'll look
at the direction of current,

296
00:15:00,008 --> 00:15:03,559
what an open circuit guarantees,
and the reverse of what we did today:

297
00:15:03,639 --> 00:15:06,800
finding the charge that passed
from the current graph.

